### The stories: title and summary (shown in the game menu). The title
### and summary of each chapter are in the file of its story.

story-eraser = The Eraser
    .summary = A creature is erasing everything in the kingdom. Moving around, creating, searching, permissions and sudo, then backups.
story-datacenter = The Data Centre
    .summary = A site down, a new recruit, a night of reboots, a fire, an expired certificate: logs, pipes |, accounts and groups, systemd, apt, nginx, backups, TLS.
story-packet = The Lost Packet
    .summary = "The internet doesn't work any more": an investigation, hop by hop, with ping, dig, traceroute and curl.
story-time-machine = The Time Machine
    .summary = Inès's manuscript is damaged: travel through its versions with git.
story-archives = The Archives
    .summary = Save a library's collection onto a small USB stick, then find the right version of a manuscript: du, df, find, tar, restic.
story-letters = The Neighbourhood Letters
    .summary = A brand-new server, already under attack: harden SSH, the firewall, fail2ban; then host two sites, DNS, survive the night of the concert, move SSH without locking yourself out, hunt down repeat offenders, and take in a site that runs on Apache.
story-fablab = The Workshop
    .summary = The neighbourhood fablab moves to containers: docker, volumes, docker compose, the firewall Docker bypasses, and containers that no longer run as root.
story-gazette = The Gazette
    .summary = The neighbourhood's online newspaper learns to deploy: git push, hooks, and rolling back when things break.
story-game-library = The Game Library
    .summary = The server of the neighbourhood game library: ransomware, backups that hold up, and a database to rescue properly.
story-on-call = On Call
    .summary = Nights on call for Neighbourhood Radio: full disk, memory, inodes, clock… then monitoring that warns you before things break, and the disk that saturates every afternoon.
story-community-centre = The Community Centre
    .summary = A gateway and an internal network behind it: your own SSH keys, ssh -J, tunnels, scp and rsync between machines.
story-coop = The Co-op
    .summary = The neighbourhood food co-op: accounts for the people who arrive and leave, shared permissions, a well-tuned sudo, the till's website and its updates, by hand and then automatic.
story-hive = The Hive
    .summary = The network of a community space: VLANs to separate offices, guests and equipment, DHCP, NAT and a firewall between zones, a VPN, a DMZ for the website, a second site, a directory and enterprise Wi-Fi.
story-audit = The Audit
    .summary = The bakery's insurer wants an audit: read the letter of engagement and take an inventory with nmap without leaving the scope, fix an auditor's findings (SSH, accounts, sudo, secrets), close what is exposed on the network, trace an unexpected login, then prove with nmap and Lynis that everything holds.
story-lab = The Lab
    .summary = The photo club's server: a disk wearing out, what SMART really says, software RAID, replacing a disk without losing anything or picking the wrong disk, growing the space with LVM, then getting out of emergency mode.
story-watch = The Watch
    .summary = Six months after the audit, the bakery has migrated to a new server: building the habit of a security watch, sorting a bulletin of CVEs, fixing what matters, proving it holds, then automating what can be automated.
story-junk-mail = Junk Mail
    .summary = The Community Gardens' emails don't arrive, or end up in junk folders: SPF, DKIM and DMARC, with Postfix, OpenDKIM and DNS.
story-fleet = The Fleet
    .summary = Three neighbourhood centres, three servers, one person: manage them together with Ansible (inventory, playbook, idempotence, --check).
story-patch = The Patch
    .summary = The bike workshop has a new server, with Caddy, which "does HTTPS by itself"… provided you give it the right name and let the certificate authority through. A short chapter to compare with nginx and certbot.
story-canteen = The Canteen
    .summary = The solidarity canteen's bookings app won't come back after a reboot, gives up after a database restart, waits two minutes for a missing cable, writes before its disk is there: systemd's dependencies, Restart=, a slow boot and RequiresMountsFor=.
story-observatory = The Observatory
    .summary = The astronomy club's server crawls under the night's photo stacking: the load average, top and renice, then a CPUQuota= so the host doesn't throttle the machine.
story-theatre = The Theatre
    .summary = The neighbourhood theatre's ticket office and its PostgreSQL database: connections exhausted on opening night (pg_stat_activity, idle in transaction, a timeout), then a slow query (EXPLAIN, an index).
story-huts = The Huts
    .summary = A federation of mountain huts and the valley's server: the huts' logs gathered with rsyslog, a journal that fills the disk, and an alert for failed SSH logins.
story-photo-club = The Photo Club
    .summary = A photo club's NAS and its darkroom computer: an NFS share and its mount, the uids that NFS carries, and a Samba share for the members' laptops.
story-bastion = The Bastion
    .summary = A year after The Watch, the Bakery's delivery server talks all night to a mining pool: find what runs and how it got in, contain it without locking yourself out, remove what was left behind and check what was changed, then harden so it can't happen again.
story-other-unix = The Other Unix
    .summary = The film club's server runs FreeBSD, and Debian habits break: a script written with GNU's tools, services without systemd (pkg, rc.conf, service), and a firewall where the last rule wins (pf).
story-old-sun = The Old Sun
    .summary = The seed library's catalogue has lived since 2009 on a Sun box running Solaris 10: read the system before touching it (SMF, ZFS), move its database to a new Debian server with a dump (byte order, character sets), then switch over and keep the old box frozen.
story-night-batch = The Night Batch
    .summary = For thirty years, a COBOL batch collected the Mutual's contributions every night on a mainframe; it moves to Linux. Read the bureau's EBCDIC file without damaging its packed amounts, compile the program with GnuCOBOL and read its return code, then fix the plumbing when the file arrives late.
story-order-book = The Order Book
    .summary = Since 1996, a flour mill has taken its bakeries' orders on an IBM i. A night job waits for a reply nobody gave, printed output nobody prints fills the disk, and the night's save left the orders out: work with jobs, messages, spooled files and save files in CL, then export the orders to Linux in the right character set.
story-mixed-fleet = Next Door Windows
    .summary = An architects' office runs a Linux server next to a Windows file server: a script written on Windows that bash can't run, the Windows share mounted on Linux the right way, and a look inside the Windows server through its OpenSSH and PowerShell.
story-music-school = The Music School
    .summary = A community music school's file server is a Windows Server, and you are its new admin, over ssh and PowerShell: the teachers can't save in their share (share and NTFS permissions), the booking app's service won't start after its account's password was changed, its forgotten debug logs fill the data volume, and the nightly backup has failed in silence for weeks.
story-card-catalogue = The Card Catalogue
    .summary = Three public libraries share one Active Directory domain, and you take it over from the domain controller, in PowerShell: a new librarian's account with the rights of his job (AGDLP), a leaver closed without being deleted, an account that locks every morning because a task on the file server still holds an old password, a Group Policy that reaches only half the people it is for, and a yearly audit: a door left open to the Internet and the accounts nobody uses any more.
story-pantry = The Pantry
    .summary = A community grocery keeps the list of the households it helps on a small Debian server, and a backup disk leaves the building every Friday. The disk spends a weekend in the tram's lost property, unencrypted: encrypt it with LUKS, give the restic repository a key that doesn't live on the server, then stage the server's loss and restore everything on a borrowed laptop with nothing but the treasurer's envelope.
story-ferry = The Ferry
    .summary = An island's ferry cooperative runs its site, its bookings and its DNS zone on a small Debian server, and the island's internet has just gone IPv6. The same services now travel two roads: an address that moved with a new network card, a firewall that only guarded IPv4, and a site that only half answers in IPv6.
story-bike-share = The Bike Share
    .summary = A neighbourhood bike share runs its rental app and its docking stations on two small Debian servers, and a third one, gauge, was supposed to watch them with Prometheus. Make it see everything, teach it when to raise the alarm, and make sure the right person hears about it, but not during the maintenance everybody knew about.
story-pool = The Pool
    .summary = The town's swimming pool moved its booking app to a small Kubernetes cluster, k3s on a single Debian server, and nobody quite knows how it works yet. A release that won't start, a site the cluster doesn't serve, a password in the wrong place, a swim meet that runs out of memory: learn to read pods, Deployments, Services and Ingresses, Secrets, requests and limits, and to fix things in the manifests where they belong.

story-staff-room = The Staff Room
    .summary = A primary school prints everything, from registers to report cards, through a small Debian server running CUPS. Unblock Monday morning's queue, add a driverless printer for the staff only and an old one with its driver, then teach the server to forget what it shouldn't keep.
story-airwaves = The Airwaves
    .summary = Five community radio stations share a Debian server and their own certificate authority, made with openssl years ago. An expired certificate, machines that don't trust the root, a link missing from a chain, a stolen laptop with its client certificate: learn how an internal PKI really works, and keep it healthy.
story-tool-library = The Tool Library
    .summary = Forty community tool libraries run Lendshed, an open-source lending software whose code lives on a small self-hosted forge, with its CI. Someone rewrote its history with an old maintainer's token, its pipeline leaked a deploy key, and a fake release circulates: close the doors, rebuild the pipeline so that contributors' code never meets a secret, and make every release verifiable from the first commit to the last byte.
story-health-centre = The Health Centre
    .summary = A rural health centre runs its accounting, its appointment book and its medical records as virtual machines on one Debian hypervisor, KVM and libvirt. After a storm, one doesn't come back and freezes when started; then the reception can't reach the new patient portal and sometimes sees a training copy instead of the real appointments; then everything swaps and a disk is full. Learn what a virtual machine really is: a definition, disk images and their chains, a network, and memory you share.
story-blood-drive = The Blood Drive
    .summary = A regional blood service books its donors online through two load balancers that share one address. The spare doesn't take over when the service dies, a sick web server keeps receiving donors, and after a night of network work both load balancers think they are in charge: learn how keepalived (VRRP) and HAProxy really keep a site up, and how they fail.
story-choir = The Choir
    .summary = A community choir keeps its members' data on a small Debian server, and the board once wrote down how long it would keep it. Logs kept since 2027, a member who asks for everything the choir holds about her, another who asks to be forgotten: personal data and its retention, from the sysadmin's side.
story-allotments = The Allotments
    .summary = An allotments association books its plots, its tools and its watering slots through a small app on a MariaDB server. Connections that never leave and a query that reads millions of rows, an app given the keys to everything, a replica that silently stopped following: running a database, from the sysadmin's side.
story-lifeboat = The Lifeboat
    .summary = A volunteer lifeboat station receives its mail on a small Debian server: Postfix, Dovecot and rspamd. A mailbox that bounces everything, a tide of spam that buries the safety bulletin the crew needs, and the winter bookings to sort while the treasurer is away: running the inbound side of mail, from the sysadmin's chair.
story-climbing-wall = The Climbing Wall
    .summary = A community climbing hall keeps its bookings and its door badges on a server behind a UPS, and a storm shows that the UPS protected nothing. A monitor watching the wrong UPS, a NAS that nobody warns, a worn battery and alerts that cry wolf: Network UPS Tools and power cuts, from the sysadmin's side.
story-dance-troupe = The Dance Troupe
    .summary = A community dance troupe keeps its website, its members' files and its films in a small self-hosted object store: a Garage cluster with an S3 API, on three machines in three places. Private documents published with the website, a nightly backup that has failed since a power cut, a big film to share for a week while a node lies dead: running object storage, from the sysadmin's side.
story-shelter = The Shelter
    .summary = Two animal shelters keep their adoption site and its database on one server in an old barn's cellar, and a disaster recovery plan nobody has read since 2029. Make the plan true, then live through the night the river comes in: rebuild on a spare machine from the backups, switch the site over, write the review, and schedule the next drill.
story-language-cafe = The Language Café
    .summary = A nonprofit's conversation tables for newcomers keep their site, the week's tables and a volunteers' sign-up app on a small server the last volunteer installed with Rocky Linux: not Debian. Find the other family's tools (dnf, firewalld and its two configurations), then meet SELinux: a site moved with mv that httpd may not read, a port it may not bind, an app it may not reach. Fix the labels, the port and the boolean, never by turning SELinux off.
story-mapping-party = The Mapping Party
    .summary = A volunteer collective that maps step-free routes keeps its DNS as code with OpenTofu. A new record written in a hurry, then an evening when someone fixed things by hand and left a run holding the lock: plan, apply, drift and import, from the sysadmin's side.
story-hostel = The Hostel
    .summary = A cooperative youth hostel keeps the passwords of its booking site in OpenBao, a vault for secrets. A night's power cut leaves it sealed; then the site turns out to read its secrets with the all-powerful root token; then its token ends up on a public forum. Unseal with key shares, write a policy, give tokens their limits, and trace a leak in the audit log.
